From 883cbbe51cf2960a2220560fcc55b5a91a371a78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 19:49:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Tutor mode: auto-explain on the page, cited sources,
 insights as AI context
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Four changes to Tutor mode, all driven by the same friction: the
explanations were there but getting to them was manual, and once read
they were gone.

Auto-explain (on by default) replaces pressing "Show on page" per
question. A MutationObserver plus a scroll listener track which question
is actually visible and float that explanation beside it, so a stepped
quiz that swaps one question for the next carries the popup with it.
Scans are debounced and gated behind a busy flag, because highlighting
and showing the popup are themselves DOM mutations and would otherwise
feed the observer that triggered them. Highlights are reused rather than
re-wrapped, and auto-explain never scrolls the page.

Explanations are substantially deeper: concept, reasoning that names why
each wrong option is wrong, a worked solution with real arithmetic shown
per step, and the common pitfall. The prompt carries a worked example of
the required detail level, since "factor; apply formula; compute" is what
you get otherwise.

Sources are cited per question. The model is told never to invent a URL
and to name the rule instead when unsure; citeList drops anything that
isn't a real http(s) link, so a javascript: or malformed URL can never be
rendered as one.

Explanations save as insights, and any insight can be switched on as
context memory with the 🧠 button on its card — it then rides along in
the system prompt of later calls, newest first, up to a character budget.
Opt-in per insight and off by default: blanket recall is noise plus
tokens on every request.

The popup is draggable by its header and remembers where it was put, so
it stays parked as questions change rather than springing back.

Verified in Chromium against a simulated stepped quiz: one popup per
question, correct switch on Next, no observer feedback loop, no nested
highlights, no scroll hijacking.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019GqTQm8E1znRkM37YVj6K7
---
